Mùgair?

51:12.000 --> 51:14.000
Mùgairing aboot.

51:14.000 --> 51:16.000
Mùigean, aye.

51:16.000 --> 51:18.000
Aye, mùigean.

51:18.000 --> 51:20.000
Aye, there was that [XX].

51:20.000 --> 51:21.000
What was that?

51:21.000 --> 51:24.000
Mùigean- [Mixing/?] among you know any,

51:25.000 --> 51:28.000
you know any kind of dirty stuff.

51:28.000 --> 51:31.000
Mùigean: ‘Don’t be mùigean about it’.

51:31.000 --> 51:33.000
Aye, you know

51:33.000 --> 51:37.000
mixing it up, mùigean.

51:37.000 --> 51:41.000
But the busach was that, a gurn [XX].

51:42.000 --> 51:43.000
Never pleased.

51:44.000 --> 51:47.000
Busach you called that.

51:47.000 --> 51:49.000
Oh, thon was [XX].

51:49.000 --> 51:51.000
What about falachan?

51:56.000 --> 51:57.000
A falachan.

52:04.000 --> 52:08.000
I think that applied to a hoard, maybe a hoard of money hidden.

52:10.000 --> 52:13.000
Something that was found , hidden and maybe found.

52:13.000 --> 52:14.000
No?

52:15.000 --> 52:20.000
Found, no. [XX]

52:21.000 --> 52:23.000
I’ve two similar ones here.

52:23.000 --> 52:25.000
I wonder if you might recognise these:

52:25.000 --> 52:28.000
a ghealachan or a gealachan?

52:28.000 --> 52:31.000
A dealachan, well that’s, that’s a light.

52:32.000 --> 52:33.000
A dealachan.

52:34.000 --> 52:38.000
Aye, a light… or the moon.

52:41.000 --> 52:42.000
Dealachan.

52:42.000 --> 52:47.000
Oh that, well right enough, gealach for moon.

52:47.000 --> 52:48.000
That’s right.

52:48.000 --> 52:53.000
They also used… they also used that for a light?

52:53.000 --> 52:57.000
That was, that was in everyday use.

52:58.000 --> 53:01.000
[XX] ghealach.

53:01.000 --> 53:05.000
This word gealachan, did that apply to a, a lamp or?

53:06.000 --> 53:08.000
No, I never heard that [one/?].

53:08.000 --> 53:09.000
Just the moon?

53:10.000 --> 53:12.000
Just the moon,  [the/?] ghealach.

53:12.000 --> 53:17.000
There could have been that in some other…

53:17.000 --> 53:20.000
There wasnae a word for the noise of gulls? No?

53:22.000 --> 53:24.000
There wasnae a word for like a,

53:24.000 --> 53:26.000
a gathering of gulls, the noise it would make?

53:29.000 --> 53:31.000
Of gulls?

53:34.000 --> 53:35.000
Well no,

53:37.000 --> 53:39.000
no’ in particular.

53:39.000 --> 53:43.000
[I would say/?] there were plenty of gulls and that

53:43.000 --> 53:47.000
but the Sooth coast, well they had a coil of gulls.

53:47.000 --> 53:48.000
A coil.

53:48.000 --> 53:49.000
Aye, a coil.

53:49.000 --> 53:51.000
They’d be sweeping around/?],

53:51.000 --> 53:54.000
they were seeing the herring although we couldna see it.

53:55.000 --> 53:56.000
A coil of gulls.

53:56.000 --> 54:01.000
[XX] but [no one/?] around us.

54:01.000 --> 54:04.000
That was an Ayrshire word.

54:06.000 --> 54:09.000
That’s fine- that seems to be everything.

54:09.000 --> 54:12.000
Aye they had, they had the gull coil.

54:12.000 --> 54:16.000
It might hae been [XX].

54:16.000 --> 54:20.000
You’ll find coil in [coil/?] of gulls.

54:20.000 --> 54:24.000
[...]

54:26.000 --> 54:31.000
A coil they called it, you know swirling aroond in a circle.

54:32.000 --> 54:35.000
[...]

54:39.000 --> 54:42.000
You didna hear of the kildee?

54:42.000 --> 54:47.000
Aye, I heard of kildee but what it is.

54:47.000 --> 54:48.000
It’s a wee fish.

54:49.000 --> 54:52.000
You used to get them in the [smoorings/?]

54:52.000 --> 54:55.000
Many a time I strung a dozen of them on a string and

54:55.000 --> 55:00.000
got a bob or more [stringing kildees/?].

55:00.000 --> 55:02.000
You know they were better than whitings

55:02.000 --> 55:05.000
but they were only wee, about that size.

55:05.000 --> 55:08.000
Only on the west loch you would get them a [XX].

55:08.000 --> 55:09.000
About a foot…?

55:09.000 --> 55:11.000
You see they would never take them off-

55:11.000 --> 55:14.000
they were just thrown on the top.

55:14.000 --> 55:17.000
I used to go through the top for the kildee.

55:18.000 --> 55:22.000
[String them up but/?] […].

55:24.000 --> 55:27.000
They weren’t [XX] among the other fish at all.

55:28.000 --> 55:30.000
That’s what they called them, kildees.

55:30.000 --> 55:31.000
Now is that an old one?

55:31.000 --> 55:32.000
That’s an old one.

55:32.000 --> 55:35.000
The old men in your youth used that word?

55:35.000 --> 55:38.000
It’s them that used it, kildees.

55:38.000 --> 55:41.000
But you’ll never hear it now.

55:41.000 --> 55:42.000
Well, they use it in Campbeltown now…

55:42.000 --> 55:43.000
Do they?

55:43.000 --> 55:45.000
At the fishing we talk about kildees, aye.

55:45.000 --> 55:48.000
You’ll no’ see them,  [XX] clear of the land.

55:48.000 --> 55:50.000
Aye well, at the trawl like, at the trawl.

55:50.000 --> 55:52.000
Do you get them?

55:52.000 --> 55:54.000
Unless that’s maybe…

55:54.000 --> 55:56.000
Well you, the next half a dozen, you try and keep them.

55:56.000 --> 55:58.000
You’ll never try better.

55:58.000 --> 55:59.000
Well that’s…

55:59.000 --> 56:01.000
They’re no’ very big, you know.

56:02.000 --> 56:05.000
Kildees that was just, they were wee.

56:05.000 --> 56:09.000
I think that’s the origination of the name,

56:09.000 --> 56:12.000
they were wee fish, kildees.

56:12.000 --> 56:16.000
I think so cos they were never very big.

56:16.000 --> 56:17.000
Big eyes though.

56:17.000 --> 56:18.000
Big eyes.

56:18.000 --> 56:20.000
Aye, it must be the same ones.

56:20.000 --> 56:21.000
That’s right.

56:22.000 --> 56:29.000
Red in colour and the kind of heavier strip [around/?].

56:29.000 --> 56:32.000
They’re more like a John Dory

56:32.000 --> 56:34.000
only on a wee-er scale.

56:36.000 --> 56:39.000
The same kind of rough scale on them.

56:39.000 --> 56:42.000
Oddly enough I was in the, I was in the company of eh…

56:42.000 --> 56:44.000
That’s the kildee then.

56:44.000 --> 56:48.000
There’s another fish… eh,

56:50.000 --> 56:51.000
was there a name,

56:51.000 --> 56:53.000
I think it’s another name for the John Dory,

56:53.000 --> 56:55.000
a local name.

56:55.000 --> 56:57.000
Can you remember that?

56:57.000 --> 56:58.000
Johnny Dories.

56:58.000 --> 57:01.000
Was it Jacob something? No, it wasn’t, no’ Jacob.

57:01.000 --> 57:02.000
Something haddy?

57:05.000 --> 57:09.000
Oh well, the John Dore, you know-

57:09.000 --> 57:13.000
Johnny Dory we call them here.

57:13.000 --> 57:16.000
John Dore in the [book/?] [XX].

57:16.000 --> 57:20.000
No, you were telling me…

57:20.000 --> 57:21.000
I tell you what it was.

57:21.000 --> 57:24.000
It was when Eric [Cregeen/?] you know the Manx man,

57:24.000 --> 57:26.000
was in. Remember?

57:26.000 --> 57:27.000
A-ha.

57:27.000 --> 57:28.000
We came together.

57:29.000 --> 57:33.000
He was talking about a type of fish they use to fish for

57:33.000 --> 57:35.000
in Tiree and they,

57:35.000 --> 57:37.000
they had the same name in Tarbert.

57:37.000 --> 57:41.000
You said they used to get them wi’ lines, fish for them wi’ lines

57:41.000 --> 57:42.000
oot o Tarbert.

57:43.000 --> 57:44.000
Hake?

57:44.000 --> 57:47.000
No, no it was a fish that’s no’, it’s no eaten now,

57:47.000 --> 57:49.000
well it’s no’ caught hardly.

57:49.000 --> 57:52.000
I think it was the John Dory but it’s something haddy.

57:54.000 --> 57:55.000
Gurnard?

57:55.000 --> 57:58.000
No, that will be the ouden or the cnòdan.

57:58.000 --> 58:01.000
Aye, cnùdan.

58:01.000 --> 58:04.000
That was our name- we called it cnùdan.

58:04.000 --> 58:06.000
A gurnard’s the right name.

58:06.000 --> 58:08.000
We used to fish them up in the [XX] burn there.

58:08.000 --> 58:09.000
Aye, cnùdan.

58:09.000 --> 58:10.000
Cnùdan.

58:10.000 --> 58:12.000
That was, when it was, when you would touch it,

58:13.000 --> 58:16.000
it would give a growl.

58:16.000 --> 58:17.000
You would feel it,

58:20.000 --> 58:24.000
you would hear the kind of syllable of a growl coming oot it.

58:24.000 --> 58:30.000
Cnùdan was our name for it but the right name is a gurnard.

58:30.000 --> 58:32.000
There weren’t talk about eh…

58:32.000 --> 58:34.000
It’s a very stabby fish.

58:35.000 --> 58:37.000
…cruchan, cnuchan dhearg?

58:38.000 --> 58:40.000
I believe there were red ones and blue ones.

58:40.000 --> 58:43.000
They wouldna distinguish between the two, no?

58:43.000 --> 58:44.000
Aye, they were red and blue.

58:44.000 --> 58:47.000
A-ha but they didna distinguish between the two in language, no?

58:47.000 --> 58:50.000
No, they were just a cnùdan, aye.

58:50.000 --> 58:56.000
You would get red ones and blue ones but it was all a cnùdan.

58:56.000 --> 59:00.000
They kinda gurned right enough when you went up to them [XX]-

59:00.000 --> 59:04.000
you would feel the vibration itself in the fish,

59:04.000 --> 59:07.000
but a very stabby fish.

59:07.000 --> 59:11.000
They used to fish them in dry wood barrels.

59:11.000 --> 59:13.000
You would get four or five shillings to pack them.

59:13.000 --> 59:14.000
Dry them?

59:15.000 --> 59:20.000
Dry wood barrels, you know the half-wooden barrels.

59:20.000 --> 59:21.000
Did they fillet them first, aye?

59:21.000 --> 59:25.000
No, they were just put in the way they fished them.

59:25.000 --> 59:29.000
They were getting [XX], I think there was five shillings for [drying/?] the barrels.

59:29.000 --> 59:30.000
This was the Mull men?

59:30.000 --> 59:34.000
Aye, fishing them up there at Duart, up that…

59:34.000 --> 59:36.000
They’d be using like wee ring nets?

59:36.000 --> 59:39.000
A ring net, that what they were using.

59:41.000 --> 59:44.000
You mentioned a word there eh,

59:44.000 --> 59:46.000
I’m interested to know whether it,

59:46.000 --> 59:47.000
whether it’s an old word,

59:47.000 --> 59:49.000
whether you can remember the old,

59:49.000 --> 59:50.000
the old men using it when you were,

59:50.000 --> 59:53.000
when you were a young man.

59:53.000 --> 59:57.000
Eh, a touch, a touch, you know, a fine touch.

01:00:00.000 --> 01:00:02.000
You know if you got a good shot, you would say a fine touch.

01:00:02.000 --> 01:00:04.000
A touch, aye.

01:00:04.000 --> 01:00:05.000
Is that an old one?

01:00:05.000 --> 01:00:06.000
That’s an old one.

01:00:06.000 --> 01:00:09.000
[XX] touch [by the old men/?].

01:00:09.000 --> 01:00:10.000
Touch they called it.

01:00:10.000 --> 01:00:11.000
You’ve no idea what that is?

01:00:11.000 --> 01:00:14.000
I’ve no idea what the…

01:00:14.000 --> 01:00:16.000
‘Aye, we had a good touch the day.’

01:00:17.000 --> 01:00:19.000
[Coming from the boats/?], you know [XX].

01:00:19.000 --> 01:00:22.000
It sounds English but whether it’s English, I don’t know.

01:00:22.000 --> 01:00:24.000
Well, the old men had that, aye.

01:00:24.000 --> 01:00:25.000
It doesn’t make any sense as English.

01:00:25.000 --> 01:00:27.000
They had it up here and back.

01:00:28.000 --> 01:00:30.000
Oh yes,

01:00:31.000 --> 01:00:33.000
when I look back and they had it

01:00:33.000 --> 01:00:35.000
‘we had a good touch’ before me,

01:00:37.000 --> 01:00:38.000
that’s over a hundred.

01:00:41.000 --> 01:00:43.000
The men’s a’ deid but

01:00:43.000 --> 01:00:46.000
many time I heard them mention the touch-

01:00:46.000 --> 01:00:49.000
‘We had a good touch the day.’

01:00:52.000 --> 01:00:57.000
Oh, them- that was [them/?],  the touch.

01:00:57.000 --> 01:01:00.000
Oh, you didna hear of the [hass/?],

01:01:03.000 --> 01:01:06.000
the [hass/?] or [has/?]?

01:01:08.000 --> 01:01:10.000
It’s just a word I heard in Campbeltown.

01:01:10.000 --> 01:01:12.000
It’s a wee class o herring.

01:01:16.000 --> 01:01:18.000
Hally?

01:01:18.000 --> 01:01:22.000
Eh, [hass/?]- [hass/?] or [haws/?].

01:01:24.000 --> 01:01:27.000
It’s herring, they’re bigger than sile but they’re

01:01:27.000 --> 01:01:31.000
no’ big,  the hass.

01:01:32.000 --> 01:01:35.000
No, no- I havna been in touch wi’ that one.

01:01:36.000 --> 01:01:38.000
That must be Campbeltown again.

01:01:38.000 --> 01:01:40.000
Aye, it must be a Campbeltown.

01:01:43.000 --> 01:01:45.000
Naw, that’s just about it.

01:01:45.000 --> 01:01:48.000
Fry, there were herring fry, you know,

01:01:48.000 --> 01:01:50.000
wee herring, sile.

01:01:50.000 --> 01:01:52.000
No, it winna be that.

01:01:54.000 --> 01:01:56.000
Sile, soil they call them now [XX].

01:01:56.000 --> 01:01:57.000
Soil?

01:01:57.000 --> 01:02:02.000
Aye, they’re wee wee, only the length of your wee finger.

01:02:02.000 --> 01:02:06.000
There’d been millions coming ashore on the beach wi’ the piuchdaidh chasing them

01:02:08.000 --> 01:02:10.000
and then put them ashore on the [wreck/?].

01:02:11.000 --> 01:02:15.000
And I seen the same thing in [Southern/?] [Italy/?].

01:02:15.000 --> 01:02:19.000
A big boat bursting wi’ them alongside the quay.

01:02:19.000 --> 01:02:22.000
There wasn’t a sile to be seen

01:02:22.000 --> 01:02:27.000
and the [XX] coming in together to all the rubbish and

01:02:27.000 --> 01:02:30.000
alongside the pier inside o her was

01:02:30.000 --> 01:02:35.000
like silver wi’ wee herring jumping out to get clear o,

01:02:36.000 --> 01:02:40.000
all the rubbish was just like silver right along the pier,

01:02:40.000 --> 01:02:43.000
with her coming in but she was [buckling/?].

01:02:45.000 --> 01:02:47.000
Aye, I used to take stock o’ all that.

01:02:49.000 --> 01:02:54.000
Just the same as here… aye.

01:02:54.000 --> 01:02:57.000
I’ve seen me up, I’d be near over the bows o’ the boat.

01:02:59.000 --> 01:03:01.000
It seemed to me big herring [playin’?],

01:03:01.000 --> 01:03:06.000
oot in the middle of the [Mediterranean/?].

01:03:06.000 --> 01:03:08.000
Oh millions of tons o’ it,

01:03:08.000 --> 01:03:10.000
[solands/?] and gulls [XX].

01:03:10.000 --> 01:03:17.000
But eh, but herring I was taking it to be.

01:03:18.000 --> 01:03:21.000
Oh, there was a sea of it just.

01:03:22.000 --> 01:03:25.000
We were st-, sailing through that for half a day.

01:03:25.000 --> 01:03:27.000
Maybe pilchards or something?

01:03:27.000 --> 01:03:30.000
It was clear, shining in the sun

01:03:30.000 --> 01:03:33.000
but it jumped just like a herring.

01:03:33.000 --> 01:03:38.000
But I seen them [XX], aye.

01:03:40.000 --> 01:03:47.000
But thon was oot [XX] efter leaving [XX].

01:03:50.000 --> 01:03:53.000
The [XX] was coming near the net o it.

01:03:53.000 --> 01:03:54.000
No, this is fine.

01:03:54.000 --> 01:03:55.000
No, take that.

01:03:57.000 --> 01:04:00.000
Oh, that’s, that’s a wee [XX].

01:04:00.000 --> 01:04:01.000
Take the half o it.

01:04:01.000 --> 01:04:02.000
No, no, no.

01:04:02.000 --> 01:04:03.000
Cut a half. I’ll get more.

01:04:03.000 --> 01:04:04.000
No, not at all.

01:04:05.000 --> 01:04:06.000
Well take a…

01:04:07.000 --> 01:04:08.000
This is fine [XX].

01:04:11.000 --> 01:04:16.000
Aye, that was in a lake, Lake [Tumshall/?] I think it was.

01:04:16.000 --> 01:04:20.000
You know there were fresh water lakes [XX].

01:04:22.000 --> 01:04:24.000
Plenty of [trout/?] if you could,

01:04:24.000 --> 01:04:26.000
well you wouldn’t get…

01:04:26.000 --> 01:04:27.000
Take a wee bit off it.

01:04:27.000 --> 01:04:29.000
Oh no, no, no, no.

01:04:29.000 --> 01:04:31.000
Aye, just a couple of smokes.

01:04:37.000 --> 01:04:39.000
But I’ll bet you there were

01:04:39.000 --> 01:04:42.000
hundreds of tons of fish on them.

01:04:44.000 --> 01:04:45.000
They were [XX]

01:04:45.000 --> 01:04:46.000
That’s the gannet?

01:04:46.000 --> 01:04:49.000
The gannet, sollan goose, aye.

01:04:49.000 --> 01:04:51.000
Was that the word the old men used?

01:04:51.000 --> 01:04:52.000
That’s the sollan.

01:04:52.000 --> 01:04:53.000
The sollan.

01:04:53.000 --> 01:04:55.000
That’s the one they [XX].

01:05:00.000 --> 01:05:02.000
You’re no familiar…

01:05:02.000 --> 01:05:05.000
That’s seemingly the right one, the sollan goose,

01:05:06.000 --> 01:05:09.000
and that’s what they called them, the sollan.

01:05:12.000 --> 01:05:15.000
Of course that’s more a Campbeltown, gannet.

01:05:16.000 --> 01:05:19.000
More a Campbeltown, the gannet.

01:05:22.000 --> 01:05:26.000
The [XX] on the Sooth coast of Ayrshire,

01:05:27.000 --> 01:05:29.000
the [Southern men/?], the sollan.

01:05:32.000 --> 01:05:35.000
Are you familiar with the word smùrach?

01:05:36.000 --> 01:05:38.000
Smùrach?

01:05:38.000 --> 01:05:43.000
Aye, that’s breakin’ anything into dust, stour.

01:05:45.000 --> 01:05:49.000
You know, anything that kind of crumbles into smùrach,

01:05:49.000 --> 01:05:50.000
wee bits.

01:05:51.000 --> 01:05:54.000
That’s the same as millean, milleans?

01:05:54.000 --> 01:05:56.000
That’s right, the same thing- smùrach.

01:05:56.000 --> 01:05:59.000
You didna hear that in connection with the weather?

01:05:59.000 --> 01:06:01.000
Wetness of the day, no?

01:06:02.000 --> 01:06:04.000
A smùrach day, no?

01:06:04.000 --> 01:06:11.000
Smùrach? Aye, [they had a word like that/?] right enough for the weather.

01:06:11.000 --> 01:06:12.000
Can you remember it?

01:06:14.000 --> 01:06:17.000
No, [just as I said/?], smùrach.

01:06:17.000 --> 01:06:22.000
Kind of smùrach, you know kind of wet, showery.

01:06:22.000 --> 01:06:23.000
A-ha. That was the word?

01:06:23.000 --> 01:06:24.000
They had that word right enough.

01:06:24.000 --> 01:06:25.000
That was the word, eh?

01:06:25.000 --> 01:06:30.000
But that said you could use it for brekkin’ it into smùrach,

01:06:31.000 --> 01:06:32.000
that’s into dust or…

01:06:33.000 --> 01:06:35.000
I wonder would that be like eh,

01:06:35.000 --> 01:06:37.000
would that be like small rain,

01:06:37.000 --> 01:06:38.000
like kind of fine rain?

01:06:38.000 --> 01:06:40.000
Small rain, kind of smùrach.

01:06:41.000 --> 01:06:42.000
Oh no, that’s right enough.

01:06:42.000 --> 01:06:44.000
That’ll be just like the English smir, I suppose?

01:06:44.000 --> 01:06:47.000
Smir, yes but they had it smùrach.

01:06:47.000 --> 01:06:49.000
Smùrach, that’s fine, eh.

01:06:50.000 --> 01:06:54.000
And then you can have the smùrach, brekkin’ it into smùrach.

01:06:56.000 --> 01:07:01.000
That’s into wee [pairts/?] as small as you can [break them/?].

01:07:02.000 --> 01:07:03.000
Smùrach.

01:07:03.000 --> 01:07:05.000
And they had that in the weather,

01:07:05.000 --> 01:07:08.000
